<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alessandro Manzoni</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1785">1785</a>–<a href="/years/1873">1873</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alessandro Manzoni was an Italian novelist and poet from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>. His historical novel The Betrothed (I promessi sposi), set in Spanish-ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a> in 1628–1630 and describing the plague of 1630, appeared in its final form in 1840–1842 and became a founding work of modern Italian literature and language. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-verdi">Giuseppe Verdi</a> composed his Requiem in Manzoni's memory.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alessandro-manzoni.jpg" alt="Alessandro Manzoni" width="320" height="422" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Francesco Hayez · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Francesco_Hayez_-_Ritratto_di_Alessandro_Manzoni.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Alessandro Manzoni was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> on 7 March 1785. His maternal grandfather was the philosopher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cesare-beccaria">Cesare Beccaria</a>, and his biological father was probably Giovanni Verri rather than Pietro Manzoni. His parents separated in 1792, and from the age of six he was schooled in religious boarding schools run by the Somaschi and Barnabite fathers. He was drawn early to poetry and to ideals of liberty and reason, and his poem The Triumph of Liberty (1801) praised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a>.</p><p>After his father's death in 1807 he joined his mother's household at Auteuil and met the Idéologues, including Claude Charles Fauriel. In 1808 he married Henriette Blondel, a Calvinist banker's daughter who became a Roman Catholic in 1810. That year Manzoni passed through a religious crisis that took him from agnosticism to an austere <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a>. His Sacred Hymns appeared in 1815.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Manzoni began Fermo e Lucia on 24 April 1821, after reading the novels of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walter-scott">Walter Scott</a> in French translation, and finished the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> in September 1823. The reshaped novel, The Betrothed (I promessi sposi), was published a volume a year in 1825–1827. It follows two ordinary fiancés, Renzo Tramaglino and Lucia Mondella, through famine, war and plague and through corruption in Church and State in 17th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a> under Spanish rule. Real figures appear, among them Cardinal Federico Borromeo and the outlaw called the Unnamed, historically identified with Francesco Bernardino Visconti.</p><p>The novel went through 68 editions and sold over 60,000 copies in fourteen years. Manzoni's tragedy Adelchi (1822), on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>'s overthrow of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> domination in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, carried veiled allusions to Austrian rule. Later he wrote mainly essays, above all on language. In 1868 he reported to the minister Emilio Broglio, proposing that Florentine be taught in schools. He supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-unification">Italian unification</a> and became a senator in February 1860.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Manzoni outlived his first wife, who died on 25 December 1833, his second wife, Teresa Borri, six of his children and his closest friends. His eldest son, Pier Luigi, died on 28 April 1873. Manzoni had fallen on 6 January while leaving the San Fedele church, and he died of cerebral meningitis, a complication of the blow to his head, on 22 May 1873. His funeral was held in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan-cathedral">Milan Cathedral</a> with almost royal pomp, and he was buried in the Monumental Cemetery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>.</p><p>He is counted with Ugo Foscolo and Giacomo Leopardi as one of the three crowns of Italian Romanticism. The Betrothed is a symbol of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-unification">Risorgimento</a> for its patriotic message, and it was a milestone in the development of the modern and unified Italian language.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Alessandro Manzoni” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Heavy taxation, troop billeting and the plague of 1630, which killed perhaps half the population of the city and is the setting of <b>Alessandro Manzoni</b>’s novel The Betrothed, weakened Lombardy in the seventeenth century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-milan">Duchy of Milan · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Elsewhere Alexander Pushkin in Russia, Adam Mickiewicz in Lithuania and later in exile, <b>Alessandro Manzoni</b> in Milan with his novel The Betrothed, and Ralph Waldo Emerson and the Transcendentalists in New England gave the movement national forms.</blockquote><a href="/movement/romanticism">Romanticism · People</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.47,9.19&amp;year=1842" aria-label="Milan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4768 560 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Alessandro Manzoni"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5218 830h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5218 830h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.47,9.19&amp;year=1842">What was here in 1842? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
